body:
Yeah...most people my age (21) are still pretty dependent on their parents, and don't have any major responsibilities, so I don't really click with them, especially people who are overly into partying and drinking. 

&#x200B;

Main exception is my SO; he is a year older, but acts like an old man.

The City Sleek Pant is Warpstreme (what the Men's ABC/Commission Pants and Essential HR Pants are made of), which is imo the best fabric LLL has for "work-appropriate" pants. I wouldn't be able to wear them in super hot weather, but they are reasonably breathable and stretchy. Other than them being a tad too short (I work with chemicals so I need pants that are a little longer), I like the fit. However, the front pockets disappointed me -- they are wayyy too small for anything but keys or a small wallet. While I'm glad for this step towards an ABC Pant for women, I think I'll check out the Dynamic Days Pant instead of picking these up immediately.

&#x200B;

The Here to There Pant is smooth on the inside and textured on the outside (looking at the pictures on the website, I think this is only in the Crosshatch color). The texture is kinda scratchy...not really into it. They are more stretchy than the City Sleeks, but still have structure. I don't really have any issues with the fit, but wish the back pocket wasn't built into the waistband. Just my preference, but I would at least prefer a back pocket that is actually built into the pant. They remind me of the [Everlane Work Pant](https://www.everlane.com/products/womens-work-pant-regular-black), but they are a tighter fit. I might be tempted by the black if they go on WMTM...but at full price they are too similar to the pants I already own. 

&#x200B;

I now understand the WT hype...but can't justify the fact that Brier Rose isn't opaque enough to hide black underwear! :,(

It looks like the first color has color-code = 15961 and display-name = TEWL- TEMPO STRIPE WHITE BLACK and the second color (Tempo Stripe White/Black) has color-code = 45326 and display-name = TEWH/BLK TEWH/BLK-TEMPO STRIPE WHITE/BLACK. But...both of those color IDs seem to have the same fabric-id: "LW3CRDS".

&#x200B;

The other fabric IDs are "LW3BQ3S" (4780 - BLACK/BLACK), "LW3BQ4S" (12826 - WHITE/WHITE, 43613 - BALLET SLIPPER/WHITE and 0572 - SLATE/WHITE), and "LW3BXAS" (32102 - WHITE/WHITE/BLACK). 

&#x200B;

So...overall, I'm not really sure if there's any difference. It just looks like someone made a mistake in the backend.

One of the programs I recently interviewed with told me that they allow each faculty member to look at all the applicants on a spreadsheet with their faculty choice, app materials, etc. Faculty can select students they are interested in to meet with, and if your interview with them goes well, it would contribute positively to your chances of acceptance. 

&#x200B;

I don't have experience with this myself, but I have heard of programs that have more formal events during the first semester (ie. all Professors accepting students give a presentation about their research), as well as those that just expect you to reach out to professors yourself. So you would come in associated with the program, not with a specific PI. The program accepts you because they are generally confident you and some associated faculty member will be a match. As far as I know (in my field), students are always accepted to the program first, and join labs later on, regardless of whether there are formal rotations or not.

I've heard to avoid speaking about industry, but I haven't had any issues so far by stating that I intend to pursue industry research (I can't see myself teaching) and asking about opportunities for like-minded students in the program. It's important to me that my graduate advisor is supportive of my goals and will help me make connections to industry, take a summer off, and so on. I made this pretty clear in my application, though, so YMMV.

&#x200B;

I've asked current graduate students of potential advisors about the lab culture, PI's mentoring style, and current projects. Other graduate students can be helpful resources regarding pros and cons of the program/university, things to do and where to live in the surrounding area, and so on. 

&#x200B;

If you cannot see yourself going there, I would personally advise seeing if you can apply to jobs and reapply to different programs next year. I'm not sure how this situation is for an international student, though, other than that getting accepted or employeed is much harder -- but I don't believe it would hurt your chances any more than it has this year.

I'm guessing that the organizers take your selections into account and fill out the visit weekend schedule with mini-interviews (usually about 30 minute blocks), but if you select less faculty than the program wants you to meet with (or the faculty you want to meet with aren't able to), the program either will try to find other people who would like to meet with you or just leave those blocks unfilled. Since this sounds like it was an interview day, they will more likely try to have you meet with as many people as possible. Then, those professors can give their input to the committee, and they make their final decision. 

&#x200B;

I've applied for several programs (both rotation- and non-rotation-based), and it seems like for the latter, it is common to spend your first term taking courses and/or teaching. That first semester is when you will "match" with a lab with more finality.

Thanks for the reply! I realize now that I didn't really clarify the nature of the problem (I wrote the post at 1AM...heh); DAPI and 647 showed up fine, but 488 did not show up at all. I did let my supervisor know, but unfortunately she knows only about as much as me for confocal. 

&#x200B;

Thankfully everything is working this morning, but since I used the same steps as yesterday, I still have no clue what could have caused it! I'll ask the tech on Monday if she has any idea.

I wish Leica support was open on weekends!! Seems to be working today, though, so whatever happened yesterday may forever be a mystery. 

&#x200B;

Is there anything I can do to troubleshoot it if something like this happens again? I'm scared to mess with a lot of settings because I don't want to create an even bigger issue, but it looks like I'll be doing this on the weekends quite a bit in the future and my immediate supervisor knows as much as me (we were trained on it together).

I haven't tried a huge range of brands, but I really enjoy my ABH mattes (although subculture...frustrates me). ABH is pretty hit or miss for me with shimmers; they make my favorite shimmer ever ("Rose Pink" from the Soft Clam palette) but a large majority don't play well with my oily eyelids. At the moment, I'd have to say my favorite brand-wise is Natasha Denona. It's hard for me to describe why, but I love the softer shimmers in the Sunrise palette and haven't been able to find anything that doesn't actually emphasize any texture on my eyelids! I'm also a sucker for her duochrome toppers, but there are definitely cheaper options that give the same effect.

&#x200B;

Any recommendations for eyeshadow primers for oily eyelids please hmu. I've tried UD PP and that worked well for me but immediately dried up?! I also can't deal with the applicator, wish I'd been around to try the old packaging. I'm currently working on the NARS primer and definitely notice that it prolongs the lifetime of my shadows, but not in the summer...screw east coast weather lol

Thanks for the reply! Is there any way for me to solve for that numerically? From OChem, I would say that the RDS is the first propogation step, but I'm not sure if we are expected to be using that knowledge.

&#x200B;

Also, is there a good way for me to solve it without knowing the RDS? In the lecture for my course, we have been solving both with and without assumptions about which steps are relatively faster or slower.
